1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,995
Every Monday, Priya spends twenty minutes doing the same three sums before she can

2
00:00:04,995 --> 00:00:09,696
write a word: how many renewals are still open, how much premium is riding on them,

3
00:00:09,696 --> 00:00:13,151
and which ones are close enough to their date to worry about.

4
00:00:13,151 --> 00:00:17,796
That twenty minutes of counting is what you are about to replace, and if you run a

5
00:00:17,796 --> 00:00:22,497
pipeline of your own, you already know exactly which sums you do by hand every week

6
00:00:22,497 --> 00:00:22,724
too.

7
00:00:22,724 --> 00:00:24,366
Why arithmetic and not prose?

8
00:00:24,366 --> 00:00:26,122
Because of who reads the thing.

9
00:00:26,122 --> 00:00:30,653
Marcus does not read Priya's report for the sentences, he reads it for the three

10
00:00:30,653 --> 00:00:32,806
numbers and whether one of them moved.

11
00:00:32,806 --> 00:00:34,109
The prose is packaging.

12
00:00:34,109 --> 00:00:38,583
Which means the worst failure of a weekly report is not clumsy writing, it is a

13
00:00:38,583 --> 00:00:43,001
wrong number delivered fluently, and hand counting on a rushed Monday produces

14
00:00:43,001 --> 00:00:47,759
exactly that: a row missed while a phone rang, a figure carried over from last week.

15
00:00:47,759 --> 00:00:50,421
The agent's advantage here is not intelligence.

16
00:00:50,421 --> 00:00:52,291
It is that it never counts tired.

17
00:00:52,841 --> 00:00:57,503
That ceiling line reads like the one from Section 4, and that is not an accident.

18
00:00:57,503 --> 00:01:01,935
Every agent you build in this course gets the same instruction in a different

19
00:01:01,935 --> 00:01:05,849
costume: do not decide something the data did not actually tell you.

20
00:01:05,849 --> 00:01:10,626
Here the costume is a column: the sheet's own Status field is the only authority on

21
00:01:10,626 --> 00:01:15,115
whether a renewal is closed, and the agent's opinion about what looks finished

22
00:01:15,115 --> 00:01:16,209
counts for nothing.

23
00:01:16,209 --> 00:01:21,044
Keep that rule in mind through Section 7, because the one failure this agent suffers

24
00:01:21,044 --> 00:01:25,821
in this course walks straight through the gap between what a sheet says and what it

25
00:01:25,821 --> 00:01:26,166
means.

26
00:01:26,166 --> 00:01:30,943
And the trial is three weeks, three Mondays, because this agent produces one output

27
00:01:30,943 --> 00:01:35,375
a week: the unit-of-evidence logic from Agent 2, stretched to a weekly clock.

28
00:01:35,375 --> 00:01:40,037
Write your own version of this template before you open n8n, the same way you did

29
00:01:40,037 --> 00:01:40,728
for Agent 1.

30
00:01:41,278 --> 00:01:42,971
Hold onto that distinction.

31
00:01:42,971 --> 00:01:48,051
Section 8's guardrails lecture comes back to it directly: a scheduled agent needs

32
00:01:48,051 --> 00:01:53,256
the same ceiling as any other you build, precisely because nobody is standing there

33
00:01:53,256 --> 00:01:55,451
to double check it fired correctly.

34
00:01:55,451 --> 00:02:00,593
You will not be watching every Monday morning, and the job description has to hold

35
00:02:00,593 --> 00:02:01,973
even when you are not.

36
00:02:01,973 --> 00:02:04,858
Spell out what unattended changes in practice.

37
00:02:04,858 --> 00:02:10,063
Agents 1 and 2 always had a human somewhere in the moment: an inbox being worked, a

38
00:02:10,063 --> 00:02:11,568
transcript being pasted.

39
00:02:11,568 --> 00:02:16,836
This agent's first audience is nobody, and an error it makes at eight on Monday sits

40
00:02:16,836 --> 00:02:19,407
unexamined until someone reads the draft.

41
00:02:19,407 --> 00:02:24,549
Two design consequences follow, and both are already in the job description if you

42
00:02:24,549 --> 00:02:25,427
look for them.

43
00:02:25,427 --> 00:02:30,507
The output is a draft that never sends itself, so the error's blast radius is one

44
00:02:30,507 --> 00:02:34,834
wrong document in a folder, not one wrong document in Marcus's inbox.

45
00:02:34,834 --> 00:02:39,725
And the trial is a comparison, agent's numbers against Priya's own hand count,

46
00:02:39,725 --> 00:02:44,805
because with no human in the loop at run time, the checking has to be designed in

47
00:02:44,805 --> 00:02:46,247
after it, deliberately.

48
00:02:46,797 --> 00:02:51,823
This section ships C03, the weekly report template, and of the four agents it is the

49
00:02:51,823 --> 00:02:56,789
one most worth adapting beyond its own job: anything you currently summarise from a

50
00:02:56,789 --> 00:03:01,636
spreadsheet on a rhythm, a sales pipeline, a project tracker, a hiring funnel, is

51
00:03:01,636 --> 00:03:04,268
this same build with different column names.

52
00:03:04,268 --> 00:03:06,961
Keep that in the back of your mind as you go.

53
00:03:06,961 --> 00:03:10,072
Next lecture starts with the schedule and the sheet.
